SELECT $bmsq$AI calling agents: what they can do in Hindi, Tamil and Kannada$bmsq$, $bmsq$ai-calling-agents-hindi-tamil-kannada$bmsq$, $bmsq$What AI voice agents can and cannot do in Indian languages, and a practical test plan for accent, code-mixing, latency and handover before you go live.$bmsq$, $bmsq$What AI voice agents can and cannot do in Indian languages, and a practical test plan for accent, code-mixing, latency and handover before you go live.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Modern AI calling agents can hold simple, structured phone conversations in many Indian languages, including Hindi, Tamil and Kannada, such as confirming interest, asking qualifying questions and booking a callback. How well they do it varies a lot between systems, so test any agent yourself for accent, code-mixing, speed of response and handover to a human before you put it in front of customers.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: confirm which languages our agent supports before publishing, and adjust the title and this post if Hindi, Tamil or Kannada are not all supported.]</strong></p><p>This post explains what voice agents generally do, where they struggle with Indian languages, and a checklist for testing one.</p><h2>How an AI calling agent works</h2><p>A voice agent has three parts working together on every turn of the conversation:</p><ol><li><strong>Speech recognition</strong> turns what the caller says into text.</li><li><strong>A language model</strong> decides what to say next, following your script and rules.</li><li><strong>Speech synthesis</strong> turns the reply back into a spoken voice.</li></ol><p>Each part can be strong in English and weaker in other languages. A system that understands Hindi well may still pronounce Kannada place names oddly. That is why you test the whole chain, not just the brochure.</p><h2>Tasks voice agents typically handle well</h2><ul><li>Calling new leads soon after they enquire to confirm interest.</li><li>Asking a short list of qualifying questions: budget range, timeline, location, requirement.</li><li>Booking a callback or appointment into a calendar.</li><li>Reminding people about appointments and confirming attendance.</li><li>Re-engaging old leads with a simple question.</li><li>Answering common, factual questions from an approved list.</li></ul><h2>Where they still struggle</h2><ul><li>Long, emotional or complex conversations, such as complaints or negotiations.</li><li>Heavy background noise, poor networks and speakerphone calls.</li><li>Strong regional accents the speech model was not trained on.</li><li>Callers who switch languages mid-sentence in ways the system does not expect.</li><li>Questions outside the script, where the risk is a confident wrong answer.</li></ul><h2>The test plan: what to check before going live</h2><table><thead><tr><th>What to test</th><th>How to test it</th><th>What good looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accent</td><td>Have callers from different regions talk to it in each language</td><td>Understands them without repeated "sorry, can you repeat"</td></tr><tr><td>Code-mixing</td><td>Mix English words into Hindi, Tamil or Kannada sentences, as people really speak</td><td>Follows the meaning, replies naturally</td></tr><tr><td>Latency</td><td>Time the pause between you finishing and the agent replying</td><td>Short enough that the call feels like a conversation</td></tr><tr><td>Interruptions</td><td>Talk over the agent mid-sentence</td><td>It stops and listens</td></tr><tr><td>Numbers and dates</td><td>Say phone numbers, budgets, dates in the local language</td><td>Captures them correctly in the summary</td></tr><tr><td>Names and places</td><td>Use local names, areas and landmarks</td><td>Pronounces and records them correctly</td></tr><tr><td>Off-script questions</td><td>Ask something not in the script</td><td>Admits it does not know and offers a human</td></tr><tr><td>Handover</td><td>Ask to speak to a person</td><td>Transfers or books a callback straight away</td></tr><tr><td>Voice quality</td><td>Play recordings to people who were not involved</td><td>Sounds clear and polite in that language</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Run at least a few dozen test calls per language with real staff before any customer hears it. Record the failures and fix the script or settings.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Writing a script for a voice agent</h2><p>A voice script is not a WhatsApp script read aloud. Spoken sentences need to be shorter, and each turn should ask only one thing. Start with who is calling and why, in the caller's language: "Hello, this is an AI assistant calling from [Business] about the enquiry you made today." Ask permission to continue. Then ask your qualifying questions one by one, confirm the key answers back ("So you're looking at a 2BHK, moving in about six months?"), and end with a clear next step such as a booked callback. Write the script in natural spoken language, not formal textbook language, because that is how callers will answer. Ask native speakers on your team to review each language version.</p><h2>Rules and good practice</h2><ul><li><strong>Disclose it is an AI</strong> early in the call. People handle it better when they know.</li><li><strong>Only call people who expect it</strong>, such as leads who enquired and agreed to be contacted.</li><li><strong>Follow telecom rules.</strong> In India, TRAI regulates commercial calls and messages, including Do Not Disturb preferences. Check the current rules with your telephony provider.</li><li><strong>Handle recordings carefully.</strong> Tell callers if calls are recorded and store data securely.</li><li><strong>Keep a human in the loop</strong> for anything sensitive, from pricing exceptions to complaints.</li></ul><h2>Where AI calling fits in a lead system</h2><p>Calling works best alongside WhatsApp and email, not instead of them. A common pattern: the lead enquires, gets an instant WhatsApp message, and an AI call follows to qualify and book. Read <a href="/blog/how-fast-should-you-reply-to-a-lead">how fast you should reply to a lead</a> for why the first minutes matter, and <a href="/blog/lead-scoring-small-teams">lead scoring for small teams</a> for how call answers can feed a score.</p><p>You can hear a live example on our <a href="/voice-demo">voice demo</a>, and see how calling fits our system on the <a href="/services/ai-calling">AI calling page</a>.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Will customers hang up when they hear an AI?</h3><p>Some will. Many are fine if the call is short, polite, relevant to something they asked for, and offers a human when needed. Test with your own audience and watch the completion rate.</p><h3>Can one agent switch between languages on the same call?</h3><p>Some systems can detect and switch, others need the language set in advance. Test it with real code-mixed speech, because that is how most callers actually talk.</p><h3>Does an AI calling agent replace my telecallers?</h3><p>It is better seen as covering the repetitive first calls and reminders, so your team spends time on qualified, interested people. Complex sales still need people.</p><p>Want to see whether AI calling fits your leads and languages? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-10-30T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$AI calling$bmsq$,$bmsq$Voice agents$bmsq$,$bmsq$Indian languages$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$AI calling$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$ai-calling-agents-hindi-tamil-kannada$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$Lead generation for real estate: a complete playbook$bmsq$, $bmsq$real-estate-lead-generation-playbook$bmsq$, $bmsq$A step-by-step real estate lead generation playbook: offers, ad channels, WhatsApp follow-up, site visit booking and CRM tracking for builders and brokers.$bmsq$, $bmsq$A step-by-step real estate lead generation playbook: offers, ad channels, WhatsApp follow-up, site visit booking and CRM tracking for builders and brokers.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Real estate lead generation works when you treat it as a pipeline, not a campaign: a specific project offer, ads on Meta and Google aimed at people who can actually buy, instant WhatsApp follow-up, a booked site visit, and a CRM that tracks every lead to a sale. Most projects lose buyers not at the ad but in the days between enquiry and site visit, so put as much effort into follow-up as into ads.</p><p>This playbook is for builders, developers and brokers in India, and it works for other markets with small changes.</p><h2>Step 1: Build an offer people can act on</h2><p>"Luxury apartments in a prime location" is not an offer. Buyers respond to specifics:</p><ul><li>Configuration and price range: "2 and 3BHK from [price]".</li><li>Location in buyer terms: distance to the metro, IT park or school.</li><li>Stage: ready to move, under construction, launch phase.</li><li>A reason to act now: launch pricing, a site visit with pickup, a limited inventory release (only if true).</li><li>Proof: RERA registration, possession record of past projects, real photos or walkthroughs.</li></ul><p>In India, registered projects generally need to show their RERA registration number in advertising. Check your state's rules before launching ads.</p><h2>Step 2: Choose channels by buyer type</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Meta ads (Facebook, Instagram)</td><td>Creating demand for a launch; visual projects</td><td>Many low-intent form fills; needs strong qualification</td></tr><tr><td>Click-to-WhatsApp ads</td><td>Buyers who prefer chatting to forms</td><td>Needs instant replies, including evenings</td></tr><tr><td>Google search ads</td><td>Buyers already searching for a location or project type</td><td>Competition from portals and other builders on the same terms</td></tr><tr><td>YouTube</td><td>Walkthroughs and location stories</td><td>Better for awareness than direct leads</td></tr><tr><td>Property portals</td><td>Buyers actively comparing listings</td><td>Same lead often shared with several sellers</td></tr><tr><td>Channel partners and referrals</td><td>Trust and local networks</td><td>Track attribution to avoid disputes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Some countries treat housing ads as a special category with limited targeting. If you advertise to NRIs or in other markets, check what applies there.</p><h2>Step 3: Qualify at the start, not on the third call</h2><p>Use one or two qualifying questions in the form or the first WhatsApp reply: configuration, budget range and timeline. Do not ask for everything. The goal is to separate "just browsing" from "buying in the next few months" so your sales team calls the right people first.</p><h2>Step 4: Follow up within minutes</h2><p>Property buyers enquire with several projects in one evening. The first to reply with a useful brochure, a floor plan and a site visit slot usually gets the visit. Set up:</p><ul><li>An instant WhatsApp reply with the brochure and one question.</li><li>A call from your team or an AI calling agent soon after.</li><li>A follow-up sequence over two weeks for those who go quiet.</li></ul><p>The <a href="/blog/click-to-whatsapp-ads-setup-guide">click-to-WhatsApp setup guide</a> has scripts you can adapt for property enquiries.</p><h2>Step 5: Make the site visit easy</h2><ul><li>Offer two specific slots, especially weekends.</li><li>Send a location pin and a named contact the day before.</li><li>Send a reminder on the morning of the visit.</li><li>Have the sales person read the lead's chat history before they arrive.</li><li>Follow up the same evening with a summary and the next step.</li></ul><h2>Step 6: Track every lead to a booking</h2><p>Put every lead in a CRM with its source ad, qualification answers, visit status and outcome. This tells you which ads produce visits and bookings, not just form fills. Without this, you will keep funding the campaign with the cheapest leads instead of the one with the most sales.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Step 7: Plan separately for NRI buyers</h2><p>If your project attracts buyers living abroad, treat them as a separate campaign. They are in different time zones, so replies and calls need to happen in their evening, not yours. They often want video walkthroughs, clear documentation and a trusted local contact, because they cannot easily visit. Family members in India may make the site visit on their behalf, so your follow-up should include them. Run NRI campaigns in the countries where your past buyers came from, write ads that answer remote-buying concerns, and offer a video call as the first step instead of a site visit.</p><h2>Weekly checklist for a live project</h2><ul><li>Leads, qualified leads, visits booked, visits done, bookings, by campaign.</li><li>Median first-response time this week.</li><li>Which creative is tiring and needs replacing.</li><li>Questions buyers keep asking, to add to ads and FAQs.</li><li>Leads older than 14 days to move into a slower nurture sequence.</li></ul><h2>How we run it for real estate</h2><p>Our <a href="/industries/real-estate">real estate client acquisition system</a> runs ads on Meta, Google and other platforms inside your own ad accounts, staged paused until you approve. The Follow-up AI messages leads on WhatsApp the moment they respond, scores them on budget and timeline, and pushes them into your CRM. A weekly plain-English report shows what turned into visits. For how this looks over a first month, see our <a href="/blog/real-estate-leads-30-days-case-study">30-day real estate case study</a>. For budget planning, read <a href="/blog/meta-ads-cost-india">Meta ads cost in India</a>.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Are portal leads better than ad leads for real estate?</h3><p>Portal leads are often actively comparing, but they may be shared with competitors. Ad leads from your own campaigns are exclusive but need more qualification. Many developers use both and compare cost per site visit.</p><h3>How do I reduce fake or junk real estate leads?</h3><p>Add a qualifying question, use higher-intent form settings, verify phone numbers via WhatsApp, and exclude placements that produce many leads but no visits.</p><h3>Should brokers run their own ads for a builder's project?</h3><p>Only with the builder's agreement and correct project details, including RERA information. Several brokers bidding on the same project audience can also push costs up for everyone.</p><p>Want a lead plan for your next project launch? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-02T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Industry$bmsq$,$bmsq$Real estate$bmsq$,$bmsq$Lead generation$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Industry$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$real-estate-lead-generation-playbook$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$How clinics get more patients with Meta ads$bmsq$, $bmsq$clinic-patients-meta-ads$bmsq$, $bmsq$How clinics can use Meta ads to get more patients: compliant offers, local targeting, WhatsApp booking and follow-up that turns enquiries into appointments.$bmsq$, $bmsq$How clinics can use Meta ads to get more patients: compliant offers, local targeting, WhatsApp booking and follow-up that turns enquiries into appointments.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Clinics get more patients from Meta ads by promoting a clear, compliant offer such as a consultation or health check, targeting people within travelling distance, letting them book through WhatsApp, and replying within minutes. The ads are only half the job: the clinics that do well treat every enquiry like a phone call at the front desk and follow up until the appointment is booked or declined.</p><p>Healthcare advertising also comes with rules that other industries do not face, so start with those.</p><h2>First, the rules</h2><ul><li><strong>Professional rules.</strong> In India, the National Medical Commission and state medical councils set rules on how doctors may advertise. Other professions such as dentistry and physiotherapy have their own councils. Read the current guidelines that apply to you, or ask your council, before launching.</li><li><strong>Meta's health ad policies.</strong> Meta restricts ads that imply knowledge of a person's health condition ("Are you suffering from...?"), certain before-and-after images and some treatment categories. Ads can be rejected or accounts restricted.</li><li><strong>Patient privacy.</strong> Do not upload patient lists for targeting or put health details into retargeting without proper consent. Be careful what you collect on forms.</li><li><strong>Honest claims.</strong> No "guaranteed cure", no "best clinic in the city" unless you can prove it. Keep claims factual.</li></ul><h2>Offers that work for clinics</h2><p>A good offer is specific and low-risk for the patient. Examples by type of practice:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Type of clinic</th><th>Offer ideas</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dental</td><td>Check-up with a clear fee, family dental day, aligner consultation</td><td>Show the clinic and team; avoid graphic images</td></tr><tr><td>Dermatology and skin</td><td>Consultation slot with a named doctor, skin analysis</td><td>Be careful with before-and-after images and claims</td></tr><tr><td>Physiotherapy</td><td>Assessment session, back-pain programme intro</td><td>Explain what the first visit includes</td></tr><tr><td>Diagnostics</td><td>Health check packages, home sample collection</td><td>List what each package includes</td></tr><tr><td>Fertility and specialist care</td><td>Information session, first consultation</td><td>Sensitive topic: calm tone, privacy reassurance</td></tr><tr><td>Eye care</td><td>Eye test, consultation for specific procedures</td><td>Name the doctor and their qualifications</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Targeting: think in travel time</h2><p>Patients rarely travel far for routine care. Set a radius that matches how far your patients actually come from. Look at the addresses in your appointment records to decide. For specialist services people will travel further, so widen the radius. Avoid targeting by health interests in ways that break Meta's rules; a broad local audience with a well-written ad usually works better anyway.</p><h2>Creative that patients trust</h2><ul><li>Real photos of your clinic, reception and team, not stock images.</li><li>The doctor speaking to camera for 20 to 30 seconds about a common question.</li><li>Clear practical details: location, timings, parking, fees if you publish them.</li><li>Reviews shown in a way that follows platform rules and your council's guidance.</li></ul><h2>Booking: make it one tap</h2><p>Patients often message instead of calling, especially in the evening. Click-to-WhatsApp ads let them start a chat directly. Your reply should confirm what they need, offer two slots, and send a location pin. The <a href="/blog/click-to-whatsapp-ads-setup-guide">click-to-WhatsApp setup guide</a> walks through the setup.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Follow-up: where clinics lose patients</h2><p>Many clinics run ads and leave enquiries to a busy receptionist between walk-ins. Leads wait for hours and book elsewhere. Fix this with:</p><ul><li>An instant WhatsApp reply to every enquiry, day or night.</li><li>A named person responsible for leads during clinic hours.</li><li>Appointment reminders the day before and on the morning of the visit.</li><li>A gentle message to no-shows offering a new slot.</li><li>A follow-up after the visit asking about their experience.</li></ul><p>See <a href="/blog/how-fast-should-you-reply-to-a-lead">how fast you should reply to a lead</a> to measure your own response time.</p><h2>What to measure</h2><p>Track for each campaign: enquiries, appointments booked, appointments attended, and treatment started. Cost per attended appointment is a far better guide than cost per lead. A cheap campaign that fills the form with people who never turn up is expensive.</p><h2>Repeat patients count too</h2><p>New patients are only part of the picture. Recall messages for check-ups, follow-up reminders and seasonal health camps bring back people who already trust you, at little cost. Ask patients during their visit whether they would like reminders on WhatsApp, record that consent, and send only useful, relevant messages. A well-run recall list often supports your ad results, because returning patients refer family members.</p><h2>How we help clinics</h2><p>Our <a href="/industries/clinics">client acquisition system for clinics</a> runs Meta and Google ads in your own ad accounts, staged paused for your approval so you can check every ad against your council's rules. The Follow-up AI replies to enquiries on WhatsApp the moment they come in, offers appointment slots and sends reminders. Leads are scored and pushed into your CRM, and you get a weekly plain-English report.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Are doctors allowed to run Facebook ads in India?</h3><p>Rules on doctors advertising are set by the National Medical Commission and state councils, and they have changed over time. Read the current guidelines or check with your council before advertising, and keep ads factual.</p><h3>Why does Meta keep rejecting my clinic's ads?</h3><p>Common reasons include wording that implies the viewer has a condition, before-and-after images, or restricted treatment categories. Rewrite the ad to talk about your service, not the viewer's health.</p><h3>Should a clinic use Google ads or Meta ads?</h3><p>Google search catches people already looking for a treatment nearby. Meta builds demand and works well for check-ups and packages. Many clinics use both; compare cost per attended appointment.</p><p>Want a patient acquisition plan that fits your clinic and your council's rules? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-04T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Industry$bmsq$,$bmsq$Clinics$bmsq$,$bmsq$Meta ads$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Industry$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$clinic-patients-meta-ads$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$Lead generation for interior designers$bmsq$, $bmsq$interior-designer-lead-generation$bmsq$, $bmsq$How interior designers can get more qualified leads from ads, Instagram, builders and referrals, and filter out browsers before the site visit.$bmsq$, $bmsq$How interior designers can get more qualified leads from ads, Instagram, builders and referrals, and filter out browsers before the site visit.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Interior designers get the best leads by reaching homeowners close to possession or renovation, showing real finished projects, and qualifying on budget and timeline before booking a site visit. Instagram and Meta ads create interest, Google search catches people ready to hire, and builder partnerships and referrals bring the warmest leads, but all of them need fast WhatsApp follow-up to convert.</p><p>The biggest complaint from interior firms is not "no leads". It is "too many people who just want ideas". This guide focuses on getting serious buyers and filtering out browsers early.</p><h2>Where interior design leads come from</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Lead quality</th><th>Volume</th><th>Best use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Referrals from past clients</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>Ask at handover; make it easy</td></tr><tr><td>Builder and society partnerships</td><td>High, well-timed</td><td>Medium in bursts</td><td>New projects near possession</td></tr><tr><td>Google search ads</td><td>Medium to high</td><td>Medium</td><td>"Interior designer in [area]" searches</td></tr><tr><td>Meta and Instagram ads</td><td>Mixed</td><td>High</td><td>Showcasing projects, packages, offers</td></tr><tr><td>Organic Instagram and Pinterest</td><td>Mixed</td><td>Slow build</td><td>Portfolio and trust</td></tr><tr><td>Google Business Profile</td><td>Medium to high</td><td>Steady</td><td>Local searches and reviews</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Timing: catch people at possession</h2><p>The best moment to reach a homeowner is the months around getting the keys. Look for projects in your city that are nearing possession and target those areas. Partner with builders to be the recommended interior option. For renovation work, target older neighbourhoods and run campaigns around times people typically plan home changes in your region.</p><h2>Show real work, not renders only</h2><p>Renders look perfect. Buyers know that. What builds trust is finished work with real light, real materials and the client's actual home. In your ads and portfolio:</p><ul><li>Show a walkthrough video of a completed home.</li><li>Show the same room as design and as delivered, if you have permission.</li><li>Mention the home type and size so viewers can relate it to theirs.</li><li>Show your process: site measurement, design, material selection, execution, handover.</li><li>Include a short clip of a client talking, only with written permission.</li></ul><h2>Qualify before the site visit</h2><p>Site visits and measurements take hours. Do not send a designer to someone who wanted free ideas. Ask in the form or first WhatsApp message:</p><ul><li>Is this a new home or a renovation?</li><li>Configuration and approximate size.</li><li>Which rooms or scope: full home, kitchen and wardrobes, specific rooms.</li><li>Budget range, using ranges rather than an open box.</li><li>When do you get possession, or when do you want to start?</li></ul><p>Use the answers to decide who gets a site visit, who gets a phone consultation first, and who gets a nurture sequence.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><p>Treat the first conversation as part of the service. A designer who asks thoughtful questions about how the family lives, cooks and stores things comes across as an expert before any drawing is made.</p><h2>Offers that bring serious buyers</h2><ul><li>A package price range for a typical home size, so people self-select.</li><li>A design consultation with a named senior designer.</li><li>A visit to your experience centre or a completed home.</li><li>A transparent cost guide showing what drives budget up or down.</li></ul><p>Avoid "free design" offers unless you want a lot of people collecting ideas to take elsewhere.</p><h2>Follow-up is a long game</h2><p>Interior decisions take weeks or months, and couples often decide together. After the first consultation, keep in touch with useful messages: material comparisons, a similar finished project, a reminder of the timeline to possession. The <a href="/blog/whatsapp-follow-up-messages-that-book-demos">WhatsApp follow-up scripts</a> adapt well to design consultations. Since many of your buyers are also property buyers, the <a href="/blog/real-estate-lead-generation-playbook">real estate lead generation playbook</a> has useful ideas about timing around possession.</p><h2>Partner with builders and allied trades</h2><p>Some of the warmest interior leads never see an ad. They come from people who meet homeowners at the right moment: builders handing over keys, society managers, real estate brokers, modular kitchen showrooms, electricians and carpenters. Approach a few of them with something useful, such as a simple handover guide for new owners that includes your contact, or a design day at a newly completed tower with the builder's permission. Agree in writing how referrals are tracked and thanked, so there is no confusion later. Keep a short list of partners and check in with each every month or two. A single good partner in a large new project can matter more than a month of broad ads.</p><h2>Measure the right numbers</h2><p>Track leads, qualified leads, consultations, site visits, proposals sent and projects signed, by source. Interior projects have high value, so a channel that produces fewer leads but more signed projects is the winner, even if its cost per lead looks high.</p><h2>How we help interior firms</h2><p>Our <a href="/industries/interior-designers">client acquisition system for interior designers</a> runs ads on Meta, Google and other platforms in your own accounts, staged paused until you approve. The Follow-up AI asks your qualifying questions on WhatsApp the moment a lead responds, scores them on budget and timeline, and pushes them into your CRM so designers only visit the serious ones.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Is Instagram enough to get interior design clients?</h3><p>Instagram builds trust and portfolio visibility, but organic reach alone is often slow and unpredictable. Pair it with paid ads and Google search for steady enquiries.</p><h3>Should I show prices in my ads?</h3><p>Showing a starting range filters out mismatched budgets and saves site visits. You do not need exact quotes; ranges by home size work well.</p><h3>How do I stop people taking my designs to cheaper contractors?</h3><p>Charge for detailed design, or credit a design fee against the project if they sign. Share full drawings only after commitment.</p><p>Want fewer browsers and more signed projects? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-06T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Industry$bmsq$,$bmsq$Interior design$bmsq$,$bmsq$Lead generation$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Industry$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$interior-designer-lead-generation$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$How CA firms can get clients online$bmsq$, $bmsq$ca-firms-get-clients-online$bmsq$, $bmsq$How chartered accountant firms can get clients online within professional rules: Google, LinkedIn, useful content, webinars and WhatsApp follow-up.$bmsq$, $bmsq$How chartered accountant firms can get clients online within professional rules: Google, LinkedIn, useful content, webinars and WhatsApp follow-up.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>CA firms get clients online mainly by being easy to find and easy to trust: a complete Google Business Profile, a clear website listing services, useful content that answers tax and compliance questions, LinkedIn for business owners, and fast follow-up when someone enquires. Before any paid promotion, check the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India (ICAI) rules on advertising and solicitation, because they limit what members can do.</p><p>This guide is for CA firms and related finance practices such as tax consultants and company secretaries. Adapt it to your own professional body's rules.</p><h2>Start with the rules</h2><p>ICAI's Code of Ethics places limits on how chartered accountants in practice can advertise and solicit clients. The guidance has been updated over the years, including on websites and online presence. Read the current version or ask the institute before you run ads, publish fee information or send promotional messages. Where the rules restrict direct promotion, the approaches below that focus on knowledge-sharing and being findable become even more important.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: have a practising CA review this section for accuracy against the current ICAI guidelines before publishing.]</strong></p><h2>Channels that work for finance firms</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Who it reaches</th><th>Effort</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Google Business Profile</td><td>Local searches for CA services</td><td>Low</td><td>Reviews and accurate categories matter</td></tr><tr><td>Website with service pages</td><td>People comparing firms</td><td>Medium</td><td>One page per service, plain language</td></tr><tr><td>Educational content</td><td>Owners with a specific question</td><td>Medium, ongoing</td><td>GST, ITR, company registration, audits</td></tr><tr><td>LinkedIn</td><td>Founders, CFOs, business owners</td><td>Medium</td><td>Share insights, not sales pitches</td></tr><tr><td>Webinars and workshops</td><td>Owners facing a deadline or change</td><td>Medium</td><td>Budget updates, new compliance rules</td></tr><tr><td>Referral partners</td><td>Clients of lawyers, bankers, consultants</td><td>Relationship time</td><td>Clear, ethical arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>Paid search or social ads</td><td>High-intent searches</td><td>Budget</td><td>Only within professional rules</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Make your website answer the questions buyers ask</h2><p>Most people looking for a CA are trying to solve one problem: register a company, file GST returns, respond to a notice, get an audit done. Create one page per service that explains, in plain language, who it is for, what documents are needed, the typical timeline and how to start. Avoid jargon. A business owner should understand the page without calling you.</p><h2>Content that brings enquiries</h2><ul><li>Deadline reminders with a short explainer: what is due, who must file, what happens if you miss it.</li><li>"What to do if you get a notice for..." guides.</li><li>Comparisons: sole proprietorship vs LLP vs private limited, old vs new tax regime.</li><li>Checklists: documents for ITR, GST registration, startup compliance in year one.</li><li>Short videos explaining one change after each budget.</li></ul><p>Every piece should end with an easy next step, such as booking a short consultation.</p><h2>LinkedIn for business clients</h2><p>Founders and finance heads spend time on LinkedIn. Share practical posts, comment usefully on their posts and connect with people in industries you know well. If you do direct outreach, keep it relevant and respectful. See <a href="/blog/linkedin-outreach-b2b-what-to-send">LinkedIn outreach for B2B</a> for message structure and timing.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Follow-up and onboarding</h2><p>A person searching for a CA often contacts several firms on the same day. Reply quickly, ask what they need and by when, and give a clear next step: a document checklist or a short call. Once they are clients, WhatsApp reminders about deadlines and pending documents keep the relationship active and lead to referrals.</p><h2>Reviews and reputation</h2><p>Before hiring a CA, most people check reviews and ask friends. Make it easy for satisfied clients to leave a review on your Google Business Profile by sending a direct link after a successful filing or audit. Reply to every review politely, including critical ones, without sharing any client details. Keep your profile, website and LinkedIn consistent: the same firm name, address, services and contact number. Small inconsistencies make people wonder whether they have found the right firm.</p><h2>Checklist for the next 30 days</h2><ul><li>Read the current ICAI guidance on advertising and online presence.</li><li>Complete your Google Business Profile and ask five clients for reviews.</li><li>Create or update one page per main service.</li><li>Publish two practical articles on upcoming deadlines.</li><li>Set a target for replying to new enquiries.</li><li>Agree a referral arrangement with one law or consulting firm.</li></ul><h2>Choosing between paid channels</h2><p>If you do use paid promotion within the rules, search ads tend to suit urgent needs such as notices and registrations, while social ads suit awareness and events. Read <a href="/blog/google-ads-vs-meta-ads-service-businesses">Google ads vs Meta ads for service businesses</a> for how to decide.</p><h2>How we help finance firms</h2><p>Our <a href="/industries/ca-finance">client acquisition system for CA and finance firms</a> runs campaigns in your own ad accounts and stages every campaign paused until you approve it, so you can review it against your professional rules. The Follow-up AI replies to enquiries on WhatsApp and email, and a weekly plain-English report shows what produced consultations.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Can a CA firm run Google ads?</h3><p>That depends on the current ICAI rules on advertising. Check the latest guidance or ask the institute before running any paid campaign, and keep the wording factual.</p><h3>What content brings the most enquiries for CA firms?</h3><p>Content tied to deadlines, notices and decisions people must make, such as choosing a business structure or tax regime, tends to attract people who need help now.</p><h3>How can a small CA practice compete with large firms online?</h3><p>By being specific. Focus on a few services or industries, answer questions better than anyone else locally, and reply faster. Large firms rarely respond to a small business enquiry within minutes.</p><p>Want an online client plan that respects your professional rules? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-09T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Industry$bmsq$,$bmsq$CA and finance$bmsq$,$bmsq$Professional services$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Industry$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$ca-firms-get-clients-online$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$Coaching institute admissions: an ads and WhatsApp plan$bmsq$, $bmsq$coaching-institute-admissions-ads-whatsapp$bmsq$, $bmsq$A season-by-season ads and WhatsApp plan for coaching institutes: reach students and parents, book demo classes, and convert enquiries into admissions.$bmsq$, $bmsq$A season-by-season ads and WhatsApp plan for coaching institutes: reach students and parents, book demo classes, and convert enquiries into admissions.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Coaching institutes fill batches by planning around the admission calendar: build awareness before results and exam dates, run ads that offer a demo class or scholarship test during peak season, and use WhatsApp to answer students and parents instantly and remind them until they attend. The demo class is usually the turning point, so the whole plan should aim to get people into one.</p><p>This plan works for exam coaching, school tuition, skill courses and language institutes. Adjust the dates to your own batch calendar.</p><h2>The admissions calendar</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>When</th><th>Main goal</th><th>Ads</th><th>WhatsApp</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Warm-up</td><td>Weeks before results or the new academic year</td><td>Build awareness and a lead list</td><td>Free resources, toppers' strategies, faculty videos</td><td>Send the resource, ask about their exam and year</td></tr><tr><td>Peak</td><td>Around results and batch launches</td><td>Book demo classes and scholarship tests</td><td>Demo class, test dates, batch start dates</td><td>Instant reply, slot booking, reminders</td></tr><tr><td>Decision</td><td>After demo or test</td><td>Convert to admission</td><td>Retarget attendees with batch details</td><td>Counsellor follow-up, fee options, parent call</td></tr><tr><td>Late intake</td><td>After batches start</td><td>Fill remaining seats</td><td>Late batch or weekend batch</td><td>Re-contact earlier leads</td></tr><tr><td>Off-season</td><td>Between cycles</td><td>Stay remembered</td><td>Low budget awareness</td><td>Useful updates, not pressure</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Speak to two audiences: students and parents</h2><p>Students care about results, faculty, peers and how the classes feel. Parents care about safety, track record, fees, distance and how progress is reported. Run separate ads for each, or at least make sure your follow-up answers both. Many admissions are decided by parents after the student shows interest, so involve them early.</p><h2>Ad offers that work</h2><ul><li><strong>Free demo class</strong> with a specific date, subject and faculty name.</li><li><strong>Scholarship or assessment test</strong> that gives the student a useful result whatever they decide.</li><li><strong>Free study material</strong> such as a formula sheet, previous-year paper analysis or a study timetable.</li><li><strong>Batch launch</strong> with a start date and a limited number of seats, if the limit is real.</li></ul><p>Be careful with claims. Do not promise ranks or selections. Use only results you can prove, and follow platform rules on advertising to minors.</p><h2>The WhatsApp journey</h2><ol><li><strong>Instant reply:</strong> "Hi, thanks for your interest in [course]. Which exam are you preparing for, and which year will you appear?"</li><li><strong>Value:</strong> send the promised material or demo details.</li><li><strong>Booking:</strong> offer two demo class slots.</li><li><strong>Reminder:</strong> the day before and two hours before the class, with location or joining link.</li><li><strong>After demo:</strong> ask how it went, share batch and fee details, offer a counsellor call with the parent.</li><li><strong>No-show:</strong> offer the next demo slot.</li></ol><p>For more message ideas, adapt the <a href="/blog/whatsapp-follow-up-messages-that-book-demos">15 WhatsApp follow-ups that book demos</a>. Since many enquiries arrive in the evening, after school, automated first replies help a lot.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Use your existing students</h2><p>Current and past students are your most believable advertisement. With permission from students and parents, share short videos of how a class actually runs, what a doubt session looks like, or a student explaining how they plan their week. Ask current families to bring a friend to a demo class. Referrals from parents who are happy with progress reports tend to arrive with trust already built, which shortens the counsellor's job considerably.</p><h2>Budget across the season</h2><p>Do not spread your ad budget evenly over the year. Spend lightly in warm-up to build a list, most heavily in peak, and selectively in late intake. Plan your budget from seats to fill, demo-to-admission rate and affordable cost per admission. <a href="/blog/meta-ads-cost-india">Meta ads cost in India</a> shows the formula.</p><h2>Counsellor follow-up</h2><p>Even with good automation, admissions usually close with a human conversation. Give counsellors a clear list each morning: who attended a demo yesterday, who asked about fees, who has not replied. Ask them to note objections so you can answer them in ads and FAQs next season.</p><h2>Handling the fee conversation</h2><p>Fees are the most common reason families delay a decision. Do not hide them until the last call; vague answers create suspicion. Share the fee structure clearly after the demo, along with what is included: study material, tests, doubt sessions, progress reports for parents. If you offer instalments or scholarships, explain the conditions in writing. Train counsellors to ask what is really behind a fee objection. Sometimes it is the amount, but often it is doubt about whether the student will keep up or whether the batch timing works. Each of those has a different answer. Keep a short written list of common objections and approved responses, and update it every season.</p><h2>Measure by batch</h2><ul><li>Leads by source and course.</li><li>Demo classes booked and attended.</li><li>Admissions and fee collected.</li><li>Cost per admission by campaign.</li><li>Most common reasons people did not join.</li></ul><h2>How we help institutes</h2><p>Our <a href="/industries/education">client acquisition system for education</a> runs ads in your own ad accounts, staged paused for your approval. The Follow-up AI replies on WhatsApp the moment a student or parent responds, books demo slots and sends reminders, and leads are scored into your CRM so counsellors call the right families first.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Should coaching institutes target students or parents with ads?</h3><p>Usually both. Students often start the enquiry, and parents make or approve the decision. Run separate messages for each group and make sure counsellors speak to both.</p><h3>When should we start ads for the next academic year?</h3><p>Start building awareness well before results and batch launches, so you already have a list of interested students when peak season begins. Peak spending should match your batch start dates.</p><h3>Are demo classes better online or offline?</h3><p>Offline demos show the campus and build parent trust. Online demos are easier to attend. Many institutes offer both and let families choose.</p><p>Want an admissions plan built around your batch calendar? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-11T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Industry$bmsq$,$bmsq$Education$bmsq$,$bmsq$WhatsApp$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Industry$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$coaching-institute-admissions-ads-whatsapp$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$How to get clients in Dubai as a small business$bmsq$, $bmsq$how-to-get-clients-in-dubai-small-business$bmsq$, $bmsq$How small businesses in Dubai can get clients: Google, Instagram and Meta ads in English and Arabic, LinkedIn for B2B, WhatsApp follow-up and networking.$bmsq$, $bmsq$How small businesses in Dubai can get clients: Google, Instagram and Meta ads in English and Arabic, LinkedIn for B2B, WhatsApp follow-up and networking.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Small businesses in Dubai get clients by combining strong local visibility (Google Business Profile and reviews), targeted ads on Google, Instagram and Meta in the languages your buyers use, LinkedIn for B2B, and very fast WhatsApp follow-up. Dubai is diverse and competitive, so be specific about which community and which area you serve rather than trying to reach everyone.</p><p>This guide is for small service businesses, consultants, clinics, trades and agencies operating in Dubai and the wider UAE, including founders expanding from India.</p><h2>Understand who you are selling to</h2><p>Dubai's population includes many nationalities and languages. A cleaning service in a residential community, a B2B software reseller and a beauty clinic have completely different buyers. Before spending on ads, write down:</p><ul><li>The nationality and language mix of your best clients.</li><li>The areas or communities they live or work in.</li><li>Whether they decide alone, with family, or as part of a company.</li><li>Which platforms they actually use for discovering services.</li></ul><h2>Channels for Dubai small businesses</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Best for</th><th>Tip</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Google Business Profile</td><td>Local services, clinics, salons, trades</td><td>Pick the right area; collect reviews steadily</td></tr><tr><td>Google search ads</td><td>Urgent needs and high-value services</td><td>Use area names in keywords and ads</td></tr><tr><td>Instagram and Meta ads</td><td>Consumer services, lifestyle, food, beauty</td><td>Show real locations and people; test languages</td></tr><tr><td>Click-to-WhatsApp ads</td><td>Any business that books via chat</td><td>Reply fast, including evenings and weekends</td></tr><tr><td>LinkedIn</td><td>B2B services, consulting, tech</td><td>Target by job title, company size and UAE location</td></tr><tr><td>TikTok and Snapchat</td><td>Younger consumer audiences</td><td>Short, native-style videos</td></tr><tr><td>Networking and business groups</td><td>B2B and professional services</td><td>Free zone communities, chambers, industry events</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Language and creative</h2><p>English works for many audiences in Dubai, but Arabic ads can perform differently with Arabic-speaking buyers, and some services reach South Asian or other communities better in Hindi, Urdu, Malayalam or Tagalog. Do not guess. Test two language versions of the same offer and compare cost per qualified enquiry. Use native speakers for translation; machine-translated Arabic is easy to spot.</p><h2>Licensing and advertising rules</h2><p>Make sure your trade licence covers the activities you advertise, and check the UAE's rules on advertising and content, including rules for influencer and paid promotions. Regulated sectors such as healthcare, finance and real estate have extra approval requirements. Your free zone authority or licensing department can tell you what applies to you.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>WhatsApp is often the default</h2><p>Many people in the UAE use WhatsApp to contact businesses. Put a WhatsApp button on your site and profile, run click-to-WhatsApp ads, and set up instant replies. If you message UAE numbers through the WhatsApp Business Platform, Meta prices those messages at the rate for the recipient's country, so check the current rate card. The <a href="/blog/click-to-whatsapp-ads-setup-guide">click-to-WhatsApp setup guide</a> covers the steps.</p><h2>Weekends, hours and response time</h2><p>Set your ad schedule and response hours around when your buyers are free, including the UAE weekend. Leads that arrive when nobody is working should still get an instant reply with a clear next step.</p><p>Also plan around Ramadan and major holidays, when routines, working hours and buying patterns shift. Adjust your ad schedule, tone and response hours accordingly.</p><h2>B2B in Dubai</h2><p>For business clients, relationships and reputation matter. Combine LinkedIn outreach with in-person events and introductions. Keep messages short, specific and respectful. Our guide to <a href="/blog/linkedin-outreach-b2b-what-to-send">LinkedIn outreach for B2B</a> covers message timing and structure.</p><h2>Trust signals that matter in Dubai</h2><p>Buyers in Dubai compare many providers, and new businesses have to earn trust quickly. Show your trade licence details where appropriate, a physical address or service area, clear prices or price ranges, and real reviews. Respond to enquiries in a professional, consistent tone, and confirm details in writing on WhatsApp or email after each call. For B2B, a short list of the types of companies you have worked with (only with permission) and a well-kept LinkedIn company page help. If you are expanding from India, make it clear who your local contact is and which time zone your team works in.</p><h2>Checklist for your first 60 days</h2><ul><li>Confirm your licence covers what you will advertise.</li><li>Complete Google Business Profile with the correct area and categories.</li><li>Collect reviews from your first clients.</li><li>Run one Google or Meta campaign with a clear offer, in two language versions.</li><li>Set up WhatsApp with instant replies.</li><li>Attend two relevant networking events.</li><li>Track every enquiry's source and outcome.</li></ul><h2>How we help businesses in Dubai</h2><p>BoostMySites runs ads on Meta, Google, LinkedIn, TikTok, YouTube and Snapchat inside your own ad accounts and billing, staged paused until you approve. The Follow-up AI replies on WhatsApp and email, and leads are scored into your CRM. There is no retainer: you buy credits (2,500 credits cost $9.99) and they are used only when something is done. Results are not guaranteed. See our <a href="/locations/dubai">Dubai page</a> for details.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do I need Arabic ads to get clients in Dubai?</h3><p>Not always. It depends on your buyers. Many services sell well in English, but if Arabic speakers are part of your market, test Arabic versions written by native speakers.</p><h3>Which platform works best for small businesses in Dubai?</h3><p>For local services, Google Business Profile and Google search often bring the highest intent. For consumer services, Instagram and Meta ads build demand. For B2B, LinkedIn and networking matter most.</p><h3>Can I run UAE ads from an Indian business account?</h3><p>Ad platforms let you target other countries from an existing account, but you should hold the right UAE licence to provide the service there. Check with your licensing authority.</p><p>Want a client plan for your Dubai business? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-13T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Country$bmsq$,$bmsq$Dubai$bmsq$,$bmsq$UAE$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Country$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$how-to-get-clients-in-dubai-small-business$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$LinkedIn outreach for B2B: what to send and when$bmsq$, $bmsq$linkedin-outreach-b2b-what-to-send$bmsq$, $bmsq$A simple LinkedIn outreach sequence for B2B: what to send in the connection request, follow-ups and the meeting ask, with timing and example messages.$bmsq$, $bmsq$A simple LinkedIn outreach sequence for B2B: what to send in the connection request, follow-ups and the meeting ask, with timing and example messages.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Good B2B LinkedIn outreach is a short sequence spread over two to three weeks: a personal connection request with no pitch, a thank-you with one relevant question, a useful resource a few days later, and only then a specific, low-pressure meeting ask. Send to a small, well-defined list of people who match your ideal client, and stop as soon as someone says no.</p><p>Most LinkedIn outreach fails because it starts with a pitch to a stranger. The fix is not a cleverer pitch. It is a better order of messages and a much better list.</p><h2>Before you send anything: the list</h2><p>Write down your ideal client in one sentence: industry, company size, location, job title and the problem you solve. Then build a list of 50 to 100 people who fit exactly. A small, accurate list beats thousands of loosely matched names, and it keeps your account within LinkedIn's limits on invitations.</p><h2>Fix your profile first</h2><p>Every prospect will click your profile before replying. Make sure it answers "why should I talk to this person?" within a few seconds.</p><ul><li>A clear photo and a headline that says who you help and how, not just your job title.</li><li>An About section written for buyers, not recruiters.</li><li>A featured section with one case study, guide or short video.</li><li>Recent activity: a few posts or thoughtful comments.</li></ul><h2>The sequence: what to send and when</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Action</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Day 0</td><td>View their profile, read recent posts, leave a genuine comment if relevant</td><td>Become a familiar name</td></tr><tr><td>Day 1</td><td>Connection request with a short personal note, no pitch</td><td>Get accepted</td></tr><tr><td>On acceptance</td><td>Thank you plus one relevant question</td><td>Start a conversation</td></tr><tr><td>Days 4–6</td><td>Share one useful resource tied to their situation</td><td>Give value first</td></tr><tr><td>Days 10–12</td><td>Specific, low-pressure meeting ask</td><td>Book a call</td></tr><tr><td>Days 18–21</td><td>Polite final message leaving the door open</td><td>Close the loop</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Example messages</h2><h3>Connection request</h3><p>"Hi [Name], saw your post on [topic]. I work with [type of company] in [region] on [area]. Would be good to connect."</p><h3>After they accept</h3><p>"Thanks for connecting, [Name]. Curious: how are you handling [specific challenge] at [Company] right now?"</p><h3>Value message</h3><p>"Thought this might be useful given what you mentioned about [challenge]: [one-line description of resource]. No need to reply, just sharing."</p><h3>Meeting ask</h3><p>"If it's useful, I could walk you through how [similar company type] approached [problem] in a 20-minute call. Would next Tuesday or Wednesday afternoon work?"</p><h3>Final message</h3><p>"I'll leave it here so I'm not cluttering your inbox. If [problem] becomes a priority later, happy to help."</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Timing tips</h2><ul><li>Send during working hours in the prospect's time zone.</li><li>Avoid sending every message on the same weekday and hour; it looks automated.</li><li>If someone replies, stop the sequence and have a real conversation.</li><li>If they decline, thank them and remove them from the list.</li></ul><h2>What not to send</h2><ul><li>A pitch in the connection request.</li><li>A long paragraph about your company's history.</li><li>"Just bumping this up" with nothing new.</li><li>Fake familiarity: "Loved your recent post" when you did not read it.</li><li>Calendar links before the person has shown any interest.</li></ul><h2>Personalise without faking it</h2><p>Personalisation does not mean writing an essay for every prospect. It means one true, specific line that shows you looked: a recent post, a hiring announcement, a new office, a product launch. Spend a minute per prospect finding it. If you cannot find anything specific, use a relevant detail about their industry instead of inventing a compliment. Group your list by industry or role so the rest of the message can be shared across similar prospects while the first line stays personal.</p><h2>A note on automation tools</h2><p>LinkedIn's user agreement restricts many third-party tools that automate actions on the site, and accounts that send too much too fast can be restricted. Check LinkedIn's current rules before using any automation, keep volumes reasonable, and write every message so it would make sense if read by the person it is addressed to.</p><h2>Measure the sequence</h2><p>Track acceptance rate, reply rate, positive reply rate and meetings booked for each version of your messages. Change one thing at a time. Leads who reply positively should go into your CRM with a score so the warmest get attention first. See <a href="/blog/lead-scoring-small-teams">lead scoring for small teams</a> for a simple model. If you sell professional services, <a href="/blog/ca-firms-get-clients-online">how CA firms can get clients online</a> shows how LinkedIn fits alongside other channels.</p><h2>How we run LinkedIn outreach</h2><p>Our <a href="/services/linkedin-outreach">LinkedIn outreach service</a> builds a targeted list, writes the sequence with you, and can be combined with LinkedIn ads run in your own ad account. Replies are followed up and scored into your CRM, and you see a weekly plain-English report.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Should I add a note to LinkedIn connection requests?</h3><p>A short, genuine note usually helps because it shows you are not sending in bulk. Keep it to one or two sentences and do not pitch.</p><h3>How many follow-ups on LinkedIn are acceptable?</h3><p>Three or four messages after connecting, each with a clear purpose and spread over a few weeks, is reasonable. Stop immediately if they ask you to.</p><h3>Are LinkedIn ads better than outreach?</h3><p>They do different jobs. Ads reach many people in a target group and build familiarity. Outreach starts one-to-one conversations. Used together, outreach messages often land better with people who have already seen your ads.</p><p>Want a LinkedIn outreach plan for your ideal clients? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-16T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$LinkedIn$bmsq$,$bmsq$B2B$bmsq$,$bmsq$Outreach$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$LinkedIn$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$linkedin-outreach-b2b-what-to-send$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$Lead scoring for small teams: a simple model$bmsq$, $bmsq$lead-scoring-small-teams$bmsq$, $bmsq$A simple lead scoring model for small sales teams: score fit, intent and timing, sort leads into hot, warm and cold, and review the model monthly.$bmsq$, $bmsq$A simple lead scoring model for small sales teams: score fit, intent and timing, sort leads into hot, warm and cold, and review the model monthly.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Lead scoring for a small team can be as simple as giving each lead points for fit (are they the right kind of buyer?), intent (what have they done?) and timing (when do they want to start?), then calling the highest scores first. You do not need complex software to begin: a spreadsheet and ten minutes of agreement with your sales team is enough, and you can automate it later.</p><p>The point of scoring is not precision. It is making sure your best leads get a fast, personal reply while weaker ones get a slower, automated path.</p><h2>The three parts of a score</h2><ul><li><strong>Fit:</strong> does this person match the clients you serve well? Budget, location, service needed, business size.</li><li><strong>Intent:</strong> how engaged are they? Replied to a message, asked about price, booked a call, opened a proposal.</li><li><strong>Timing:</strong> when do they need it? This month, this quarter, "just exploring".</li></ul><h2>A starter scoring model</h2><p>The points below are a starting point to adapt, not a formula proven for every business. Change the criteria to match your own sales.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Signal</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fit</td><td>Budget in your normal range</td><td>+20</td></tr><tr><td>Fit</td><td>Located in an area you serve</td><td>+10</td></tr><tr><td>Fit</td><td>Needs a service you are strong in</td><td>+10</td></tr><tr><td>Fit</td><td>Outside your service area or budget</td><td>−20</td></tr><tr><td>Intent</td><td>Replied to your first message</td><td>+15</td></tr><tr><td>Intent</td><td>Asked about price, availability or process</td><td>+15</td></tr><tr><td>Intent</td><td>Booked a call or visit</td><td>+25</td></tr><tr><td>Intent</td><td>No reply after several messages</td><td>−10</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Wants to start within a month</td><td>+20</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Within three months</td><td>+10</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>"Just exploring" or no timeline</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Turn scores into actions</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Band</th><th>Example threshold</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hot</td><td>60 and above</td><td>Personal call or WhatsApp from a senior person the same day</td></tr><tr><td>Warm</td><td>30 to 59</td><td>Follow-up sequence plus a call within a couple of days</td></tr><tr><td>Cold</td><td>Below 30</td><td>Automated nurture messages; re-score if they engage</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The thresholds are examples. After a month, look at which bands actually produced clients and move the lines.</p><h2>How to collect the data without annoying leads</h2><p>You only need a few answers. Ask one or two qualifying questions in the ad form and the rest naturally in the first WhatsApp conversation: "Roughly what budget are you working with?" "When are you hoping to start?" Record the answers straight into your CRM or sheet. If you use AI calling, the call can collect the same answers; see <a href="/blog/ai-calling-agents-hindi-tamil-kannada">what AI calling agents can do</a>.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Worked example: scoring two leads</h2><p>Here is how the starter model above sorts two example leads. These are made-up leads, used only to show the method.</p><p><strong>Example lead A</strong> filled a form with a budget in your normal range (+20), lives in your service area (+10), needs your core service (+10), replied to your first WhatsApp message (+15), asked about availability (+15) and wants to start this month (+20). Total: 90. That is a hot lead. A senior person should call today.</p><p><strong>Example lead B</strong> gave a budget below your range (−20), lives in your area (+10), needs your core service (+10), has not replied to two messages and has no timeline (0). Total: 0. That is a cold lead. Put them into a slow nurture sequence and re-score if they reply.</p><p>Without scoring, a busy team might call lead B first simply because the form arrived earlier. With scoring, the person most likely to buy gets attention while they are still interested.</p><h2>Keep scores fresh</h2><ul><li><strong>Decay:</strong> reduce intent points for leads who have gone quiet for a few weeks.</li><li><strong>Re-score on activity:</strong> a cold lead who replies "is this still available?" should jump straight up.</li><li><strong>Review monthly:</strong> compare scores with outcomes. If many "cold" leads are buying, a criterion is wrong.</li><li><strong>Ask sales:</strong> the people talking to leads will tell you which signals really matter.</li></ul><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Too many criteria. Start with five or six.</li><li>Scoring on things you cannot verify, such as job titles typed carelessly into a form.</li><li>Never contacting cold leads at all. Low score means slower path, not no path.</li><li>Letting hot leads wait. Scoring only helps if hot leads get a fast reply; read <a href="/blog/how-fast-should-you-reply-to-a-lead">how fast to reply to a lead</a>.</li></ul><h2>Agree the model with your team</h2><p>A scoring model only works if the people using it trust it. Sit down with whoever calls leads and agree on five or six signals and their points in one short meeting. Write the model at the top of your sheet or in your CRM settings so everyone sees the same rules. When someone disagrees with a score, record the case and review it at the end of the month rather than overriding the model quietly. Over time, those disagreements are exactly what improves it.</p><h2>From spreadsheet to automation</h2><p>A spreadsheet works for a few dozen leads a month. Beyond that, scores should update automatically as leads reply, book or go quiet. In the BoostMySites system, leads from ads and follow-ups are scored and pushed into the CRM, and the Follow-up AI's conversations feed the score. See our <a href="/services/leads-crm">leads and CRM service</a> for how it works.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do small businesses really need lead scoring?</h3><p>If you get more leads than you can call personally the same day, yes. Scoring decides who gets your limited time first. If you only get a handful a week, call them all quickly instead.</p><h3>Should lead scoring be manual or automatic?</h3><p>Start manual to learn what signals matter, then automate once the model is stable. Automatic scoring saves time and reacts faster when a lead's behaviour changes.</p><h3>What is the difference between lead scoring and lead qualification?</h3><p>Qualification decides whether a lead is a possible buyer at all. Scoring ranks qualified leads so you know who to contact first.</p><p>Want a scoring model built for your sales process? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-18T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$CRM$bmsq$,$bmsq$Lead scoring$bmsq$,$bmsq$Sales process$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$CRM$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$lead-scoring-small-teams$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$7 signs your marketing agency is wasting your money$bmsq$, $bmsq$signs-marketing-agency-wasting-money$bmsq$, $bmsq$Seven warning signs your marketing agency is wasting your budget, from vanity reports to account ownership, and the questions to ask before you renew.$bmsq$, $bmsq$Seven warning signs your marketing agency is wasting your budget, from vanity reports to account ownership, and the questions to ask before you renew.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Your marketing agency may be wasting your money if its reports focus on reach and clicks instead of qualified leads and sales, if you do not own your ad accounts, or if nobody can explain in plain English what changed last month and why. One sign alone may just need a conversation; three or more usually means it is time to renegotiate or move.</p><p>Many agencies do excellent work. This is not an attack on agencies. It is a checklist so you can tell the difference and get what you pay for.</p><h2>1. Reports talk about impressions, not enquiries</h2><p>Reach, impressions, clicks and followers are inputs. They matter only if they lead to enquiries and sales. If your monthly report is full of charts but never says how many qualified leads came in, how many booked, and what each cost, ask for it. If the agency cannot provide it, they are not measuring what matters to you.</p><h2>2. You do not own your ad accounts</h2><p>If your ads run in the agency's ad account or business manager, your history, audiences and pixel data may stay with them when you leave. Ask: "Whose name is on the ad account, and who pays the platform directly?" The safest setup is ads running in your own accounts, with the agency given access.</p><h2>3. Nobody asks what happened to the leads</h2><p>An agency that sends leads to your inbox and never asks which ones became clients is optimising blind. Good partners want to know lead quality, because that is how they improve targeting. If yours never asks, they may be optimising for cheap leads, not good ones.</p><h2>4. The same ads have run for months</h2><p>People stop noticing ads they have seen many times. If your creatives have not changed in months and results are slipping, the account is not being actively managed.</p><h2>5. Changes happen once a month, if at all</h2><p>Ad platforms move daily. Budgets should shift toward ads that work and away from ones that do not. If the only changes happen just before the monthly review, your money is running on autopilot in between.</p><h2>6. You cannot get a straight answer</h2><p>Ask simple questions: "What did you change last week? Why? What happened?" If the answers are full of jargon or vague talk about "the algorithm", you are not being kept informed. You are paying for clarity as much as for execution.</p><h2>7. Long lock-ins or guaranteed results</h2><p>Be careful with long minimum terms that make leaving expensive, and be very careful with guarantees of leads or sales. Nobody controls the ad auction or your market. A guarantee usually means fine print, or low-quality leads counted as results.</p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Red flags vs normal growing pains</h2><p>Not every disappointing month means your agency is the problem. New campaigns need time to learn, seasonal dips happen, and sometimes the offer itself needs work. The difference is how the agency responds. A good partner explains what happened, shows the data, and proposes specific changes. A partner wasting your money explains it away, changes nothing and asks for more budget. Judge them on how they handle bad months, not just good ones.</p><h2>Quick audit checklist</h2><ul><li>I can see cost per qualified lead and cost per client for last month.</li><li>The ad accounts and billing are in my company's name.</li><li>I approve new campaigns before they go live.</li><li>Someone asks me about lead quality at least monthly.</li><li>New creatives were launched in the last few weeks.</li><li>I know what changed last week and why.</li><li>I can leave without losing my data or ad history.</li></ul><p>Tick fewer than five? Raise it with your agency. Share this checklist and ask for a plan to close the gaps.</p><h2>What to do next</h2><p>Before you switch, have an honest conversation. Some problems, such as slow follow-up on your side, are not the agency's fault. Read <a href="/blog/facebook-ads-clicks-but-no-leads">why your Facebook ads get clicks but no leads</a> to check where the leak really is. If you decide to compare options, <a href="/blog/agency-vs-in-house-vs-ai-client-acquisition-cost">agency vs in-house vs AI tool</a> breaks down the full cost of each.</p><h2>How BoostMySites is set up differently</h2><p>We built our system around these issues. Ads run in your own ad accounts and billing. Every campaign is staged paused until you approve it. An optimisation loop runs every 20 minutes, around 72 checks a day. The Follow-up AI messages leads the moment they respond, and a Report Writer sends a weekly plain-English summary of what changed and why. There is no retainer or subscription: you buy credits (2,500 credits cost ₹899 + GST in India or $9.99 elsewhere) that are used only when something is done, and we do not guarantee results. See the full <a href="/compare/vs-agency">comparison with a traditional agency</a>.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How long should I give a new agency before judging results?</h3><p>Give enough time and budget to test properly, often a couple of months, but expect clear reporting and visible changes from the first weeks. Lack of communication is a warning sign immediately.</p><h3>Is it normal for an agency to run ads in its own account?</h3><p>It happens, but it is better for you to own the account and give the agency access. That protects your data and history if you change partners.</p><h3>What should a good monthly marketing report include?</h3><p>Spend, qualified leads, bookings or sales, cost per qualified lead and per client, what changed and why, and the plan for next month, in language you can understand without a glossary.</p><p>Want an honest second opinion on your current setup? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-20T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Getting clients$bmsq$,$bmsq$Agency$bmsq$,$bmsq$Marketing budget$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Getting clients$bmsq$, false
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.blogs WHERE slug = $bmsq$signs-marketing-agency-wasting-money$bmsq$);

INSERT INTO public.blogs (title, slug, excerpt, meta_description, content, author, published_date, reading_time, tags, image, category, is_published)
SELECT $bmsq$Google ads vs Meta ads for service businesses$bmsq$, $bmsq$google-ads-vs-meta-ads-service-businesses$bmsq$, $bmsq$Google ads capture people already searching; Meta ads create demand among people who are not. How service businesses can choose, test and combine both.$bmsq$, $bmsq$Google ads capture people already searching; Meta ads create demand among people who are not. How service businesses can choose, test and combine both.$bmsq$, $bmsq$<p>Google ads are usually better when people actively search for your service, because you reach them at the moment of need. Meta ads are usually better when people do not search but respond to a good offer, because you can put your service in front of the right audience before they start looking. Most service businesses benefit from both, with the budget weighted toward whichever matches how their buyers behave.</p><p>The question is less "which platform is better" and more "how do my buyers find services like mine?"</p><h2>The core difference</h2><p>Google search captures existing demand. Someone types "physiotherapist near me" or "GST registration consultant" and sees your ad. Meta (Facebook and Instagram) creates or awakens demand. Someone scrolling sees your ad for a home interior package and thinks "we should sort out our flat".</p><h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Google ads</th><th>Meta ads</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Buyer state</td><td>Actively looking</td><td>Not necessarily looking</td></tr><tr><td>Targeting</td><td>Keywords, location, schedule</td><td>Location, demographics, interests, lookalikes</td></tr><tr><td>Creative</td><td>Mostly text, plus assets</td><td>Images, videos, reels, carousels</td></tr><tr><td>Lead capture</td><td>Website, call, lead forms</td><td>Instant forms, WhatsApp, Messenger, website</td></tr><tr><td>Lead intent</td><td>Often higher</td><td>Often lower; needs qualification</td></tr><tr><td>Volume</td><td>Limited by how many people search</td><td>Can scale to large audiences</td></tr><tr><td>Main risk</td><td>Paying for irrelevant searches</td><td>Cheap, low-quality leads</td></tr><tr><td>Main skill</td><td>Keyword and negative keyword control</td><td>Offer, creative and follow-up</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which suits your service?</h2><ul><li><strong>Urgent needs</strong> (repairs, emergency care, legal notices): Google first.</li><li><strong>Considered local services</strong> (clinics, salons, tuition): both, often Google for steady demand and Meta for offers and packages.</li><li><strong>Visual, aspirational services</strong> (interiors, weddings, fitness, real estate): Meta first, Google for branded and high-intent searches.</li><li><strong>B2B services</strong> (accounting, IT, consulting): Google for searched services, LinkedIn for targeting by role, Meta selectively.</li><li><strong>New categories people do not search for yet</strong>: Meta or other social platforms, because there is little search demand to capture.</li></ul><h2>How to test fairly</h2><ol><li>Use the same offer on both platforms so you compare channels, not offers.</li><li>Track every lead's source into one CRM or sheet.</li><li>Follow up every lead with the same speed and process.</li><li>Run long enough to get a meaningful number of leads from each.</li><li>Compare cost per qualified lead and cost per client, not cost per click.</li></ol><p><strong>[EDITOR: insert our real account data here if available — a side-by-side of cost per qualified lead on Google vs Meta for one anonymised service client, with date range.]</strong></p><p><strong>[EDITOR: add one real example and screenshot here — client name only with written permission]</strong></p><h2>Landing pages differ too</h2><p>The same landing page rarely suits both platforms. A Google searcher already knows what they want, so the page should confirm you offer exactly that, show price or process, and make contact easy. A Meta visitor was interrupted while scrolling, so the page needs to explain the problem, build trust with real photos and reviews, and offer a low-commitment next step such as a WhatsApp chat. Keep both pages short and fast on mobile, and match each one to the ad that sends people there.</p><h2>Using both together</h2><p>The two platforms help each other. Someone sees your Meta ad, does not click, and a week later searches for your service on Google, where your search ad or brand listing catches them. Retargeting can close the loop the other way. Budget-wise, a common pattern is to fund search to cover the demand that exists, then add Meta to grow beyond it. Use your own numbers to decide the split; <a href="/blog/meta-ads-cost-india">Meta ads cost in India</a> shows how to work backwards from your margins.</p><h2>Mistakes on each platform</h2><h3>Google</h3><ul><li>Using broad keywords without negatives and paying for job seekers or DIY searches.</li><li>Sending all ads to the homepage.</li><li>Ignoring the search terms report.</li></ul><h3>Meta</h3><ul><li>Optimising for clicks instead of leads.</li><li>Weak offers and stock images.</li><li>Slow follow-up on form and WhatsApp leads. See <a href="/blog/facebook-ads-clicks-but-no-leads">why Facebook ads get clicks but no leads</a>.</li></ul><h2>Other platforms worth knowing</h2><p>Google and Meta are the usual starting points, but they are not the only options. YouTube suits services that need explaining, such as treatments, courses or property walkthroughs, and can be run from your Google Ads account. LinkedIn ads cost more per click in many markets but let you target by job title and company, which is hard to do elsewhere for B2B. TikTok and Snapchat reach younger audiences with short, native-style video. Newer formats such as ads inside AI assistants like ChatGPT are emerging; treat them as small tests until you have your own data. The same rule applies to all of them: one offer, consistent tracking, the same fast follow-up, and judgement based on cost per qualified lead.</p><h2>How we run both</h2><p>Our <a href="/services/ai-ad-campaigns">AI ad campaigns</a> run on Google, Meta, LinkedIn, TikTok, YouTube, Snapchat and ChatGPT ads from your own ad accounts and billing, staged paused until you approve them. The optimisation loop checks campaigns every 20 minutes and shifts budget toward what produces qualified leads, and a weekly plain-English report shows which platform is working for you.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Should a new service business start with Google or Meta?</h3><p>If people already search for your service in your area, start with Google search. If they do not, or your service is visual and offer-driven, start with Meta. Add the second once the first is measured.</p><h3>Are Google leads always better quality than Meta leads?</h3><p>Often, because the person was searching, but not always. A well-qualified Meta campaign with fast follow-up can produce excellent leads. Measure for your own business.</p><h3>Can I run both with a small budget?</h3><p>You can, but splitting a small budget too thinly makes both hard to judge. Start with one, prove it, then add the other.</p><p>Want to know which platform fits your service, with a budget split to match? <a href="/#contact-form">Get my client acquisition plan</a>.</p>$bmsq$, $bmsq${"name": "Kavya Shree.R", "avatar": "https://www.boostmysites.com/team/kavya.jpg", "bio": "Kavya Shree.R is Chief Marketing Officer at BoostMySites and runs the blog's content calendar. She writes about getting clients with ads, WhatsApp and follow-up systems for growing businesses."}$bmsq$::jsonb, '2026-11-23T03:30:00.000Z', 5, ARRAY[$bmsq$Ads$bmsq$,$bmsq$Google ads$bmsq$,$bmsq$Meta ads$bmsq$]::text[], 'https://www.boostmysites.com/brand/hero-poster.jpg', $bmsq$Ads$bmsq$, false
